!.vscode/extensions.json
.vs/
CMakeUserPresets.json
compile_commands.json
data/
*.o
*.out
EOF_IGNORE

# ============================================================== source stubs
cat > src/placeholder.cpp << EOF_SRC
// Replace me: first real translation unit of the core library.
namespace ${PKG_NS} {
int keep_the_library_non_empty() { return 0; }
}  // namespace ${PKG_NS}
EOF_SRC

cat > src/main.cpp << 'EOF_MAIN'
int main() { return 0; }
EOF_MAIN

cat > tests/test_main.cpp << 'EOF_TEST'
#include <gtest/gtest.h>

TEST(Sanity, AlwaysPasses) { EXPECT_EQ(1 + 1, 2); }
EOF_TEST

cat > bench/bench_main.cpp << 'EOF_BENCH'
#include <benchmark/benchmark.h>

static void BM_Nothing(benchmark::State& state) {
    for (auto _ : state) {
        benchmark::DoNotOptimize(0);
    }
}
BENCHMARK(BM_Nothing);

BENCHMARK_MAIN();
EOF_BENCH

[ -f "$HOME/.clang-format" ] && cp "$HOME/.clang-format" . || true

# ==================================================================== README
cat > README.md << 'EOF_README'
# @NAME@

C++20 with g++, CMake, Ninja, and vcpkg, running inside WSL/Linux.
The CMake files define the build for every IDE. `run.sh` provides terminal
shortcuts; the `.vscode` files provide optional VS Code shortcuts.

## Everyday use

    ./run.sh run        # build and run the app
    ./run.sh dev        # build and run the normal tests
    ./run.sh test       # build and test with ASan + UBSan
    ./run.sh help       # show all commands

Ordinary build/run commands are incremental. The project generator performs
the initial configure and prepares clangd's compilation database for you.

## Choose your IDE

Keep the project and compiler in WSL when switching IDEs.

- **VS Code:** run `code .` from this project's WSL terminal. Use Ctrl+Shift+B
  to build, Ctrl+F5 to run, and F5 to debug. The WSL-side extensions are clangd,
  CMake Tools, and CodeLLDB. clangd handles editing; g++ compiles the program.
- **CLion:** open the same WSL project folder. Use the Ubuntu-24.04 WSL
  toolchain. Under Settings > Build, Execution, Deployment > CMake, enable
  and select the imported `dev` profile, then build/run the `app` target.
  CLion supplies its own editor and debugger integration.
- **Other CMake-capable IDEs:** select the WSL/Linux toolchain and the `dev`
  configure/build preset. Editors using clangd can read `.clangd`, which
  points to `build-dev/compile_commands.json`.

The four portable VS Code configuration files are included in Git so they
remain available after cloning. Personal IDE state, `.idea`, build outputs,
and `CMakeUserPresets.json` remain ignored.

## Build without an IDE or helper script

    cmake --preset dev
    cmake --build --preset dev
    ctest --preset dev
    ./build-dev/app

Run the configure command once after cloning. CMake/Ninja handles changes to
CMakeLists and the dependency manifest during subsequent builds. After editing
presets directly, configure again. `./run.sh build` handles this check for you.

Available presets: `dev` for normal debugging, `asan` for ASan + UBSan, `tsan`
for ThreadSanitizer, and `release` for optimized builds and benchmarks.
For example, `cmake --workflow --preset asan` configures, builds, and tests.

## Requirements and dependencies

Install CMake >= 3.25, Ninja, g++, Git, and vcpkg once in your WSL distribution.
vcpkg defaults to `~/vcpkg`; export `VCPKG_ROOT` only for a different location.
The manifest already includes GoogleTest and Google Benchmark.

To add a library:

    ./run.sh add fmt
    ./run.sh setup
    ./run.sh usage fmt

Then add the suggested `find_package(...)` and `target_link_libraries(...)`
lines to CMakeLists.txt. Link libraries to `@PKG@_core` so the app, tests, and
benchmarks inherit them. Use `PUBLIC` when your public headers expose the
dependency's types; otherwise use `PRIVATE`.

Put reusable code in `src/` and public headers in `include/`. Add each new
implementation file to the `add_library(@PKG@_core ...)` source list.
`src/main.cpp` remains the app entry point.

## Occasional maintenance

    ./run.sh bench      # optimized build and benchmark run
    ./run.sh setup      # explicitly refresh the dev configuration
    ./run.sh clean      # remove build outputs; keep installed dependencies

The presets select `/usr/bin/g++`. After moving the project or deliberately
changing its compiler, run `cmake --preset dev --fresh`; repeat for any other
preset you use. These defaults target WSL/Linux. Native Windows or macOS
builds need appropriate compiler paths and options.
EOF_README
sed -i "s/@NAME@/$NAME/g; s/@PKG@/$PKG/g" README.md

git init -q
git add -A
git commit -q -m "scaffold: initial project structure" \
  || echo "Note: git commit skipped (set user.name / user.email, then commit)."

echo "Created $DIR"
echo "Preparing the dev build and clangd compilation database..."
if ! ./run.sh setup; then
  echo "Project created, but initial configure failed. Fix the error above, then:"
  echo "  cd $DIR && ./run.sh setup"
  exit 1
fi
echo
echo "Ready:"
echo "  cd $DIR && code ."
echo "  Ctrl+Shift+B: build | F5: debug | Ctrl+F5: run"
echo "  ./run.sh dev       # build + tests"
echo "  ./run.sh test      # asan/ubsan"
echo "  ./run.sh add fmt   # add a dependency (see README.md)"
